<div class="content">
<a name="prefs"></a>
<h2> Preferences </h2>

<p> As I mentioned above, there is a Preferences item on the Options
menu which will allow you to set various things, including the size of
those "what am I doing when I tap somewhere" circles. </p>

<p> <img src="../images/iLarn/8prefs.gif" alt="[preferences]">
<img src="../images/iLarn/8prefs2.gif" alt="[preferences]"> </p>

<p> When you have changed the numbers, you can hit the "Draw" button
to display the three areas.  It will draw the middle region in black.
(The "Clear" button will redraw the preferences screen without the
big black donut.) </p>

<p> [insert image] </p>

<p> The preferences screen also allows you to:</p>

<ul>
<li>
Turn off "running" altogether, so that you move only one square at a
time no matter how far from the center you tap.  (When you are
running, iLarn is smart enough to cut corners in twisty passages that
you have previously explored, if there are no intersecting passages.)
<li>
Turn on/off "centered" scrolling.  If on, the <b>@@</b> will tend to stay
in the center of the screen; if off, the screen will only scroll when
the <b>@@</b> is about to walk off the visible portion.  Numerous people
requested this and I have to admit that the flickering is not as bad
as I had expected.  It is off by default, so as not to surprise anyone
(if you move step-by-step, it might make you a bit motion-sick.)
<li>
Turn on/off "relative movement".  If this is on, the pie/donut is
centered around the @@; if this is off, the pie/donut is fixed in place,
centered on the screen.
<li>
Set the default font size ("big" normal font, or teeny flyspeck font.)
You can still change it at whim with '/F', but it will tend to start out
using the font size you select here.
<li>
Turn on/off sound.  There are two places to turn it off.  One is "Game
Sound" under "General" in the PalmPilot "Prefs" application.  (That
will turn off sound for all games that bother to pay attention.)  The
other is the "Sound" checkbox you see here.  If either one of these is
'off', iLarn will be silent; if you want sound, therefore, they must
both be 'on'.
<li>
Turn on/off "discretion".  (Discretion is the better part of valor.)
This affects running and is somewhat experimental.  If it is on (and
you have "running" turned on), you will run only in territory that you
are familiar with; when you are exploring new territory, you will
always walk.
</ul>

<?php include ("playnavigation.txt") ?>
</div>

<div class="content">
<a name="hwbs"></a>

<h2> Hardware Buttons </h2>

<p> This is where you can rebind the hardware buttons, if you so desire.
By default, they are not rebound: </p>

<p> <img src="../images/iLarn/8buttons.gif" alt="[buttons]">
<img src="../images/iLarn/8buttons3.gif" alt="[buttons]"> </p>

<p> You have the option of rebinding the four application buttons, the
up/down scroll buttons, and the silkscreened calculator and find
buttons.  You can rebind some, all, or none.  Once you have made some
choices, check the "use these settings" box to turn it on.  (If you
ever want to turn the rebinding off temporarily, all you have to do is
uncheck the box.  iLarn will remember your settings but won't use
them.)  There's an example above in case you're not feeling very
creative. </p>

<p> <img src="../images/iLarn/8buttons2.gif" alt="[buttons]"> </p>

<p> You have a fixed set of choices for each button: n/e/s/w movement,
up/down movement, casting a spell, resting for a turn, bringing up the
map of the level, changing the font size, listing the 'known items',
attempting to teleport, or bringing up the 'inventory' dialogs.  (The
dashed choice you see at the top is "don't rebind this button",
i.e. let the button do whatever it ordinarily would.)  Let me know if
there is some function you desperately want that does not appear as a
choice. </p>

<p> If you have read this far and still haven't decided whether to
download the game, you can take a whirlwind tour of
